From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Hold the rate steady rather than letting the picture decide it

This began as a probe. The format of every link was held still at once -
1280x720 at 30 Hz, a few megabits, and a rate held steady - so that one
question could be answered: whether what broke a cast was the size of the
picture, the room the link had for it, or the way the rate rose and fell with
how hard the picture was.

Two of the three turned out not to be the cause, and were given back: the
mode is chosen again from what the receiver offers and what is being shared,
and the rate is set per encoder from what each was measured carrying. What
broke the cast was a group sharing the lower band with the station connection
on the same radio.

The third is kept, on its own merits. The encoder was asking for a constant
quality, which means the rate follows the content: a still page costs little
and a moving one costs whatever it costs, in bursts a shared radio will not
carry. Upstream asked for a constant quantiser and called it constant bitrate
in the comment beside it, which is how a scrolling page came out at 25 Mbit/s.
A held rate is what a link sharing its radio can plan around, and every
measurement since has been made on top of it.

diff -ruN a/src/wfd/wfd-client.c b/src/wfd/wfd-client.c
--- a/src/wfd/wfd-client.c
+++ b/src/wfd/wfd-client.c
@@ -242,16 +242,36 @@
 static WfdResolution *
 wfd_client_best_mode (WfdVideoCodec *codec, gint width, gint height)
 {
-  g_autoptr(GList) preferred = wfd_video_codec_get_resolutions_cea (codec);
-  g_autoptr(GList) all = NULL;
-  WfdResolution *best = wfd_client_pick_resolution (preferred, width, height);
+  g_autoptr(GList) preferred = NULL;
+  g_autoptr(GList) all = wfd_video_codec_get_resolutions (codec);
+  WfdResolution *best = NULL;
 
-  if (!best)
+  /* TESTING ONLY. Hold every link at 1280x720 at 30 Hz, which is the format
+   * the accounts of what survives a Wi-Fi Direct link agree on, so that what
+   * a steadier one is worth here can be measured before anything is built to
+   * choose between them. A receiver that announces 720p at 60 Hz announces it
+   * at 30 Hz as well, and a source is free to send the slower of the two.
+   * Remove this patch to go back to choosing a mode. */
+  for (GList *item = all; item; item = item->next)
     {
-      all = wfd_video_codec_get_resolutions (codec);
-      best = wfd_client_pick_resolution (all, width, height);
+      WfdResolution *candidate = (WfdResolution *) item->data;
+
+      if (candidate->width == 1280 && candidate->height == 720 &&
+          candidate->refresh_rate == 30 && !candidate->interlaced)
+        {
+          g_message ("WfdClient: Testing: holding the link at 1280x720@30.");
+          return candidate;
+        }
     }
 
+  g_message ("WfdClient: Testing: the receiver does not announce 1280x720@30; choosing as before.");
+
+  preferred = wfd_video_codec_get_resolutions_cea (codec);
+  best = wfd_client_pick_resolution (preferred, width, height);
+
+  if (!best)
+    best = wfd_client_pick_resolution (all, width, height);
+
   return best;
 }
 
diff -ruN a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
--- a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
+++ b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
@@ -638,6 +638,13 @@
    * Wi-Fi link carries. */
   bitrate_kbit = MIN (bitrate_kbit, 10 * 1024);
 
+  /* TESTING ONLY. Hold the picture at 4 Mbit/s, which is what the accounts of
+   * a link that keeps going is thought to carry at 720p30, so that it
+   * can be seen whether what breaks up here is the size of the picture or the
+   * room the link has for it. Remove this patch to go back to 10 Mbit/s. */
+  bitrate_kbit = MIN (bitrate_kbit, 5 * 1024);
+  g_message ("WfdMediaFactory: Testing: holding the picture at %u kbit/s.", bitrate_kbit);
+
   /* At a higher refresh rate the same bitrate is spread over more frames,
    * each of which would get fewer bits. The limit grows with the rate, so a
    * 60 Hz frame gets as many as a 30 Hz one, still within the receiver's
@@ -729,7 +736,13 @@
                      * scrolling page came out at 25 Mbit/s and a noisy
                      * picture at 390 Mbit/s, in frames of up to 1.8 MB,
                      * which no Wi-Fi link carries without backing up. */
-                    "pass", 5,
+                    /* TESTING ONLY. 0 is GST_X264_ENC_PASS_CBR, which holds
+                     * the rate steady instead of letting it rise and fall
+                     * with how hard the picture is. A link that carries a
+                     * steady rate is said to keep going where one asked for
+                     * bursts does not, and that is what is being measured.
+                     * Remove this patch to go back to 5, constant quality. */
+                    "pass", 0,
                     "tune", 0x4, /* zero latency */
                     "speed-preset", 1, /* ultrafast */
                     "rc-lookahead", 1,
@@ -739,7 +752,11 @@
                      * A receiver that does not take several slices per
                      * picture gets one. */
                     "threads", (guint) CLAMP (codec->max_slice_num, 1, 4),
-                    "vbv-buf-capacity", 50,
+                    /* TESTING ONLY. Two frames at 30 Hz rather than one and a
+                     * half, so a frame that is hard to encode can borrow from
+                     * one neighbour instead of being crushed to fit. Still
+                     * nine times tighter than this encoder's own default. */
+                    "vbv-buf-capacity", 67,
                     /* The tools that make the high profile compress better
                      * than baseline at the same bitrate. */
                     "dct8x8", profile == WFD_H264_PROFILE_HIGH,
